{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And now flee, etc.</b> He decreed four things upon him: first, that he should not linger but should depart at that very hour — and this is his saying <b>and now.</b> Second, that he should go at a run, like one fleeing — and this is his saying <b>flee.</b> Third, that he should not have others accompany him to escort him but he alone — and this is his saying <b>to you</b> (i.e., by yourself). Fourth, that he should not go from this city to another city of the cities of Moab or Midian, but to his place, Pethor — and this is his saying <b>to your place.</b> And since all of this is a degradation to Balaam, the opposite of what he had sent him (Numbers 22:17): ״כִּי כַבֵּד אֲכַבֶּדְךָ מְאֹד״, therefore he said to him: ״I thought to honor you,״ etc. And his saying <b>and behold, HaShem has withheld you from honor</b> — meaning: everything HaShem did in not letting you curse them was only for the purpose of this very thing, which is to withhold you from honor." "And Balaam concealed his argument through falsehood, <b>Did I not also speak to your messengers, etc.</b> — meaning: even so, you thought and accepted and sent to me ״כִּי כַבֵּד אֲכַבֶּדְךָ.״ And he repeated, saying ״your messengers whom you sent,״ and did not suffice with saying merely ״your messengers,״ though it is self-evident that he sent them. Rather, by the phrase <b>your messengers</b> he intended the first messengers, and by his saying <b>whom you sent</b> he intended the messengers sent the second time. And his saying <b>saying</b> means: I did not merely speak the words to them but I instructed them to say them to you."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:13:54.668804184Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}